    content: `ETHICS and MORALITY are often used interchangeably (the words even have the same root meaning — Latin mos/mores and Greek ēthos both mean "custom" or "character"), but philosophers usually draw a helpful distinction along these lines: MORALITY is the FIRST-ORDER, practical thing: the actual code of conduct — the set of norms, values, principles, virtues, and judgements about right/wrong, good/bad, permitted/forbidden — that a particular person, group, or society actually holds and tries to live by. It is descriptive and lived: "the morality of Victorian England," "her personal morality," "a moral intuition that torturing children is wrong." ETHICS is the SECOND-ORDER, reflective thing: the systematic, philosophical STUDY of morality. It steps back and asks what makes actions right or wrong, whether moral claims can be true, where moral obligations come from, and whether a given moral code is justified or should be reformed. Ethics is traditionally divided into: METAETHICS (the nature and status of moral claims — are they objective? what does "good" mean?), NORMATIVE ETHICS (which general theory of right action is correct — consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics, contractualism), and APPLIED ETHICS (specific issues — abortion, euthanasia, animal rights, AI). "Ethics" is also the standard word for PROFESSIONAL codes of conduct: medical ethics, legal ethics, research ethics, business ethics, journalistic ethics. So a workable slogan: MORALITY is what you're studying; ETHICS is the studying of it. You can raise ethical questions about a society's morality.`,
