    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between a necessary and a sufficient condition logic if then only if A is necessary for B means B cannot occur without A A is sufficient for B means A guarantees B',
      'a NECESSARY condition for something is one that MUST be present for it to occur without it the thing cannot happen but its presence alone does not guarantee the thing having oxygen is NECESSARY for a fire but oxygen alone will not start a fire being 35 is necessary to be US president',
      'a SUFFICIENT condition for something is one whose presence GUARANTEES the thing occurs though there may be other ways to get the same result being a dog is SUFFICIENT for being a mammal but not necessary cats are mammals too scoring 100 percent is sufficient to pass',
      'in logic if A then B means A is sufficient for B and B is necessary for A A if and only if B means A is both necessary and sufficient for B a condition can be necessary but not sufficient sufficient but not necessary both or neither',
    ],
    content: `A NECESSARY CONDITION and a SUFFICIENT CONDITION describe two different logical relationships between one thing and another. X is a NECESSARY CONDITION for Y if Y CANNOT occur (or be true) UNLESS X is present. X must be there — but X being there does not, by itself, guarantee Y. In other words: no X, no Y. Examples: oxygen is necessary for combustion (no fire without it), but oxygen alone doesn't start a fire; being at least 35 years old is a necessary condition for being US President; having a valid ticket is necessary to board the flight; being unmarried is necessary for being a bachelor. X is a SUFFICIENT CONDITION for Y if X being present GUARANTEES that Y occurs (or is true) — although Y might also be achievable in other ways, so X is not the only route. In other words: if X, then Y. Examples: being a dog is sufficient for being a mammal (but not necessary — cats qualify too); scoring 100% is sufficient to pass the exam (but not necessary — 60% also passes); decapitation is sufficient for death; being divorced is sufficient for being unmarried. THE LOGIC: the conditional "if A, then B" says A is SUFFICIENT for B, and equivalently B is NECESSARY for A. The biconditional "A if and only if B" says A is BOTH necessary and sufficient for B (they stand or fall together). A given condition can be: necessary but not sufficient (oxygen for fire), sufficient but not necessary (being a dog for being a mammal), both (being a plane triangle with three equal angles, for being equilateral), or neither (being tall, for being a good basketball player). Confusing the two is a common reasoning error ("you need a degree to get hired" does not mean "a degree gets you hired").`,
