    content: `In METAPHYSICS and the PHILOSOPHY OF MIND (this is the "mind-body problem" — not the unrelated monism/dualism debate in international-law theory), DUALISM and MONISM are opposed answers to the question: how many fundamentally different kinds of thing does reality ultimately contain? DUALISM holds there are TWO basic, irreducibly different categories — the MENTAL and the PHYSICAL — and neither can be reduced to or fully explained by the other. SUBSTANCE DUALISM (René Descartes): the mind is a distinct non-physical substance — a thinking thing (res cogitans) with no spatial extension — separate from the body, a physical extended thing (res extensa). The two interact (Descartes speculated, via the pineal gland). PROPERTY DUALISM: there is only one kind of substance (physical), but it has two irreducibly different kinds of PROPERTY — physical properties and mental/phenomenal ones (e.g. the subjective feel of pain, "qualia"), the latter not reducible to the former. MONISM holds there is ultimately just ONE kind of fundamental stuff. Its main versions: PHYSICALISM (materialism) — everything that exists is physical; mental states just ARE brain states (identity theory) or functional states (functionalism) or supervene on the physical. This is the dominant view in contemporary philosophy and science. IDEALISM — everything is ultimately mental or mind-dependent; matter is a construction out of experience (George Berkeley: "to be is to be perceived"). NEUTRAL MONISM — the one fundamental kind of stuff is in itself neither mental nor physical; mind and matter are both aspects or arrangements of it (Spinoza, William James, Bertrand Russell). THE STANDING PROBLEMS: substance dualism faces the INTERACTION PROBLEM (how can a non-physical mind push physical neurons around without violating physics?); physicalism faces the HARD PROBLEM OF CONSCIOUSNESS (why is any physical process accompanied by subjective experience at all, rather than going on "in the dark"?).`,
