    id: 'kb-gap-phil-paradox-contradiction',
    title: "Paradox vs Contradiction",
    category: 'Philosophy',
    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between a paradox and a contradiction a statement that is simply false because it asserts and denies the same thing P and not P versus an apparently valid argument from apparently true premises to an unacceptable or self contradictory conclusion',
      'a CONTRADICTION is a statement or pair of statements that asserts and denies the same thing at the same time in the same respect P and not P it is necessarily FALSE by the law of non contradiction this circle is square the door is both fully open and fully closed there is nothing puzzling about it it is just impossible',
      'a PARADOX is an argument that starts from premises that all seem TRUE proceeds by steps that all seem VALID and yet arrives at a conclusion that is FALSE absurd or self contradictory the puzzle is that you seem forced to accept something unacceptable so at least one premise or step must be wrong but it is not obvious which',
      'examples the liar paradox this sentence is false the sorites paradox one grain is not a heap adding one grain never makes a heap so no heap exists Zenos paradoxes of motion Russells paradox resolving a paradox means finding the faulty assumption a veridical paradox has a surprising but true conclusion a falsidical one has a false conclusion from a hidden bad step',
    ],
    content: `A CONTRADICTION and a PARADOX are different things, though a paradox often LEADS to an apparent contradiction. A CONTRADICTION is a proposition (or a pair of propositions) that asserts and denies the very same thing, at the same time and in the same respect — schematically "P and not-P." By the LAW OF NON-CONTRADICTION, a contradiction is NECESSARILY FALSE — it cannot possibly be true. "This figure is a square circle." "The light is entirely on and entirely off right now." "Sam is taller than Alex and Alex is taller than Sam." There is nothing deep or puzzling about a contradiction; it is simply an impossibility, and spotting one is a decisive reason to reject whatever led to it (reductio ad absurdum works this way). A PARADOX is an ARGUMENT (or a situation generating one) in which: the premises all seem clearly TRUE, the reasoning all seems clearly VALID, and yet the conclusion is FALSE, absurd, or self-contradictory. The distinctive discomfort of a paradox is that you appear to be RATIONALLY FORCED into accepting something you cannot accept — which means at least one of the "obviously true" premises, or one of the "obviously valid" steps, must actually be faulty, even though it is not obvious which one. Resolving a paradox is the work of finding and rejecting that hidden bad assumption. Famous examples: the LIAR PARADOX ("This sentence is false" — if true it's false, if false it's true); the SORITES / heap paradox (one grain isn't a heap; adding one grain to a non-heap never makes a heap; therefore no number of grains is a heap); ZENO's paradoxes of motion (Achilles and the tortoise); the SHIP OF THESEUS (identity over gradual replacement); RUSSELL's paradox (the set of all sets that don't contain themselves). Philosophers distinguish a VERIDICAL paradox (the shocking conclusion is actually TRUE once you accept it — e.g. some results in probability or relativity) from a FALSIDICAL paradox (the conclusion is genuinely false, produced by a concealed fallacy) and from an antinomy (a paradox that resists any accepted resolution).`,
